WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.501 --> 00:00:03.100
-Our first guest tonight
is an Emmy-Award-winning actor

00:00:03.100 --> 00:00:06.467
you know best from his roles
in shows such as "Boston Legal,"

00:00:06.467 --> 00:00:08.934
"The Office," "The Practice,"
and, of course, "The Blacklist."

00:00:08.934 --> 00:00:11.100
The 200th episode
of "The Blacklist"

00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:13.767
airs this Sunday, March 19th,
here on NBC.

00:00:13.767 --> 00:00:15.167
Let's take a look.

00:00:15.167 --> 00:00:18.834
-Why are you in my home
at this hour? This is not okay.

00:00:18.834 --> 00:00:21.434
-You're right. It's not.
I understand that.

00:00:21.434 --> 00:00:23.501
And under any other
circumstances,

00:00:23.501 --> 00:00:25.334
I wouldn't have imposed.

00:00:25.334 --> 00:00:26.801
-Did you sleep here?

00:00:26.801 --> 00:00:30.601
-A very quick catnap,
just on the bench over there.

00:00:30.601 --> 00:00:32.467
Truly a half-hour at most.

00:00:32.467 --> 00:00:35.234
And then the time it took
to put together breakfast.

00:00:35.234 --> 00:00:39.400
You see, I was at Mount Vernon,
playing a very small part

00:00:39.400 --> 00:00:41.634
in a Revolutionary War
reenactment

00:00:41.634 --> 00:00:43.434
when I got to drinking
quite late

00:00:43.434 --> 00:00:45.634
with the 1st Delaware Regiment.

00:00:45.634 --> 00:00:47.667
One thing led to another.

00:00:47.667 --> 00:00:51.634
Those Blue Hen boys really know
how to hold their whiskey.

00:00:51.634 --> 00:00:53.667
-Please welcome back
to the show our friend

00:00:53.667 --> 00:00:55.200
James Spader, everybody!

00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:57.901
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:57.901 --> 00:01:07.267
♪♪

00:01:07.267 --> 00:01:16.601
♪♪

00:01:16.601 --> 00:01:25.901
♪♪

00:01:25.901 --> 00:01:29.801
That's James Spader.
Guys, it's very --

00:01:29.801 --> 00:01:32.601
It's very rare to get
exciting news from the guest

00:01:32.601 --> 00:01:34.834
while you walk
from there to here.

00:01:34.834 --> 00:01:37.801
But you just whispered to me
some exciting news.

00:01:37.801 --> 00:01:40.534
-I did. No, I saw Flaco live
in the park.

00:01:40.534 --> 00:01:42.000
[ Laughter ]

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:44.400
I -- I was walking one day,

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:48.701
and there were
all these people crowded

00:01:48.701 --> 00:01:51.100
around the base of this tree.

00:01:51.100 --> 00:01:54.801
And then also lenses like this.

00:01:54.801 --> 00:01:56.400
-Yeah.
-You know?

00:01:56.400 --> 00:01:58.367
And they'd been parked there,
obviously, for some time.

00:01:58.367 --> 00:02:01.901
And he was up in the tree.
And I saw him up there.

00:02:01.901 --> 00:02:03.801
I will say this, though.

00:02:03.801 --> 00:02:06.501
You know, I mean,
I don't want to,

00:02:06.501 --> 00:02:08.834
you know, send any shade...

00:02:08.834 --> 00:02:11.334
-Right.
Is this going to be anti-Flaco?

00:02:11.334 --> 00:02:12.968
-...on your bet. No, no.

00:02:12.968 --> 00:02:14.701
[ Laughter ]

00:02:14.701 --> 00:02:16.667
no. I just --

00:02:16.667 --> 00:02:19.667
I -- Really.
I'm, you know, in my --

00:02:19.667 --> 00:02:25.100
I'm 63, and just this year
for the first time,

00:02:25.100 --> 00:02:27.634
I heard, in multiple --
from multiple sources,

00:02:27.634 --> 00:02:29.701
like, reading it in all --

00:02:29.701 --> 00:02:32.968
in several different articles
just this year,

00:02:32.968 --> 00:02:35.701
including an article
about Flaco,

00:02:35.701 --> 00:02:39.200
that owls of the bird species

00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:41.434
are actually incredibly stupid.

00:02:41.434 --> 00:02:42.868
-Alright.

00:02:42.868 --> 00:02:44.767
-So that expression...
-Okay.

00:02:44.767 --> 00:02:47.701
-...of Flaco, who is like...
-Yeah.

00:02:47.701 --> 00:02:50.334
You're saying
that maybe he wasn't

00:02:50.334 --> 00:02:52.400
like, "How dare you guys,"
and that's more just how --

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:53.801
That's how he looks
all the time?

00:02:53.801 --> 00:02:55.467
-He certainly --
He certainly wasn't like,

00:02:55.467 --> 00:02:57.167
"What, do you think
I'm an idiot?"

00:02:57.167 --> 00:02:58.501
No, it wasn't that.
-Okay.

00:02:58.501 --> 00:03:00.501
-It was like, "Yeah?"
-Well, thanks for --

00:03:00.501 --> 00:03:02.367
Thanks for settin' us
straight, James.

00:03:02.367 --> 00:03:04.834
[ Laughter ]

00:03:04.834 --> 00:03:06.801
I like that, like,
all of a sudden, what are you --

00:03:06.801 --> 00:03:08.334
what are you --
Did you just start

00:03:08.334 --> 00:03:10.667
reading bird books at 63
that you're like --

00:03:10.667 --> 00:03:13.033
"Now I can't stop reading
how dumb owls are."

00:03:13.033 --> 00:03:16.167
-Yeah, I really --
I don't know how it came about,

00:03:16.167 --> 00:03:20.334
but it's amazing
how there's been such, you know,

00:03:20.334 --> 00:03:25.701
a dichotomous narrative about
owls, like, particularly --

00:03:25.701 --> 00:03:27.734
I mean, even the little pictures
you showed

00:03:27.734 --> 00:03:29.734
is like the owl as a teacher.
-Yeah.

00:03:29.734 --> 00:03:31.801
-And, you know,
it must be their sort of

00:03:31.801 --> 00:03:32.901
furry eyebrows or something.

00:03:32.901 --> 00:03:34.300
-Yeah,
but when you think about it,

00:03:34.300 --> 00:03:36.767
I mean, obviously,
everybody I know --

00:03:36.767 --> 00:03:39.434
every person I knew who slept
all day and was up all night,

00:03:39.434 --> 00:03:42.234
they were dummies, too, so --
-Yes.

00:03:42.234 --> 00:03:44.334
Amazing hearing.
-Yeah.

00:03:44.334 --> 00:03:47.100
They -- It's very nice for you
to end this on an up note.

00:03:47.100 --> 00:03:48.801
-Yes.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:48.801 --> 00:03:51.000
-I mean, they don't understand
a thing they hear.

00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:52.501
-Just doing my part.

00:03:52.501 --> 00:03:54.234
-So, 200 episodes.

00:03:54.234 --> 00:03:56.267
This is the final season
of this incredible show.

00:03:56.267 --> 00:03:57.801
Congratulations.
-Thank you. Thank you.

00:03:57.801 --> 00:04:00.100
[ Cheers and applause ]
-And -- I mean,

00:04:00.100 --> 00:04:03.234
shows don't run
like this anymore.

00:04:03.234 --> 00:04:05.100
You received a --
This is lovely.

00:04:05.100 --> 00:04:07.267
There was --
You know, sometimes I think

00:04:07.267 --> 00:04:08.767
people might not know this.

00:04:08.767 --> 00:04:10.767
When you hit a milestone,
the show will give out

00:04:10.767 --> 00:04:12.701
sort of a crew gift
to everybody.

00:04:12.701 --> 00:04:16.534
-Yes, I mean, there's enormous
amount of gift-giving, you know,

00:04:16.534 --> 00:04:19.133
around the corporate culture
of all kinds, you know.

00:04:19.133 --> 00:04:21.634
-Yeah.
-But -- And on sets, too.

00:04:21.634 --> 00:04:23.901
You know,
and it's a very strange thing,

00:04:23.901 --> 00:04:26.033
I think, in that --
You know, every --

00:04:26.033 --> 00:04:30.601
everybody earns a good living
on our show, you know?

00:04:30.601 --> 00:04:33.601
And -- But, like,
we'll give the cast,

00:04:33.601 --> 00:04:36.000
and the producers will give
the whole crew,

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:37.934
you know, a Christmas gift.
-Yeah.

00:04:37.934 --> 00:04:40.701
-This year, it was
an electronically heated vest.

00:04:40.701 --> 00:04:42.033
-Very nice. Very thoughtful.

00:04:42.033 --> 00:04:44.300
-But, you know,
we all know each other

00:04:44.300 --> 00:04:45.801
really well, you know?

00:04:45.801 --> 00:04:48.033
We all work together every day
and so on and so forth.

00:04:48.033 --> 00:04:49.767
So it's a different thing.

00:04:49.767 --> 00:04:54.534
You know, so whoever it is
who's in charge of, you know,

00:04:54.534 --> 00:04:57.400
picking out corporate gifts
from the studio

00:04:57.400 --> 00:05:01.767
or the network
to the cast members or the crew,

00:05:01.767 --> 00:05:04.367
you know, they don't
really know anybody at all.

00:05:04.367 --> 00:05:05.868
-Yeah.

00:05:05.868 --> 00:05:07.601
-And they're getting presents
for people that,

00:05:07.601 --> 00:05:12.534
as I said, you know,
can afford their things.

00:05:12.534 --> 00:05:14.767
The only person who probably
can't very well

00:05:14.767 --> 00:05:18.167
is the person who's
having to decide on that gift.

00:05:18.167 --> 00:05:19.634
-Right.
-You know, that's probably --

00:05:19.634 --> 00:05:21.234
-The one person who can
actually use the gift, right.

00:05:21.234 --> 00:05:24.167
-The one person who could
really use the gift, you know.

00:05:24.167 --> 00:05:26.667
Anyway -- [ Laughs ]

00:05:26.667 --> 00:05:29.701
I remember I did a show

00:05:29.701 --> 00:05:34.634
for some time for ABC

00:05:34.634 --> 00:05:37.000
called "Boston Legal."
-Of course.

00:05:37.000 --> 00:05:38.701
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:38.701 --> 00:05:40.901
Of course.

00:05:40.901 --> 00:05:43.167
-And, you know, it was
a fairly successful show.

00:05:43.167 --> 00:05:46.501
It won a whole bunch of Emmys,
and, you know, it did very well.

00:05:46.501 --> 00:05:49.701
And I remember once,
sort of late in the game,

00:05:49.701 --> 00:05:55.534
I got a gift at one point
from ABC,

00:05:55.534 --> 00:05:59.534
and it was a Tiffany box
from Tiffany's store.

00:05:59.534 --> 00:06:00.834
And I was, "Oh, great.

00:06:00.834 --> 00:06:02.234
This --
What's this going to be?"

00:06:02.234 --> 00:06:05.300
And I open it up,
and it was a bracelet.

00:06:05.300 --> 00:06:07.200
[ Laughter ]

00:06:11.901 --> 00:06:14.400
-Yeah.
-A silver cuff.

00:06:14.400 --> 00:06:15.968
-Yeah.
-Bracelet.

00:06:15.968 --> 00:06:18.667
-And did you immediately think,
"I'm never going to wear this"?

00:06:18.667 --> 00:06:20.067
-Well --

00:06:20.067 --> 00:06:21.801
I mean, I didn't really
even know what to do with it...

00:06:21.801 --> 00:06:23.067
-Yeah.
-...let alone wear it.

00:06:23.067 --> 00:06:25.834
But -- Anyway.
Yeah, so this year --

00:06:25.834 --> 00:06:29.200
Well, for the 100th episode,

00:06:29.200 --> 00:06:33.501
our entire cast and crew
got TVs.

00:06:33.501 --> 00:06:34.901
-Alright. Well, that's --
that's very nice.

00:06:34.901 --> 00:06:36.801
-Very nice.
-Yeah.

00:06:36.801 --> 00:06:41.067
-I mean, they were small
black-and-whites with antennas.

00:06:41.067 --> 00:06:42.634
[ Laughter ]
-Sure.

00:06:42.634 --> 00:06:44.467
-A separate VHF and UHF.
-Of course.

00:06:44.467 --> 00:06:46.534
Yeah, well, I mean,
they've got to get it

00:06:46.534 --> 00:06:47.901
for a bunch of people, you know?

00:06:47.901 --> 00:06:50.400
-A TV nonetheless.
-Yeah.

00:06:50.400 --> 00:06:51.834
-No. We got really nice TVs.

00:06:51.834 --> 00:06:53.968
-And that's a, you know,
thoughtful gift.

00:06:53.968 --> 00:06:55.267
Everybody needs one.

00:06:55.267 --> 00:06:56.701
-You know, that was
the 100th episode.

00:06:56.701 --> 00:06:59.367
So then, now think like
a hundred episodes later,

00:06:59.367 --> 00:07:03.501
so that's several seasons,
right?

00:07:03.501 --> 00:07:07.901
So, my gift arrived in my
dressing room, but it was...

00:07:10.667 --> 00:07:13.567
[ Laughter ]

00:07:13.567 --> 00:07:15.834
I always think of the --
You know, I'm --

00:07:15.834 --> 00:07:17.334
I'm --
[ Laughter ]

00:07:17.334 --> 00:07:19.601
I earn a very respectable
salary, you know?

00:07:19.601 --> 00:07:21.734
And I feel like,
you know, you --

00:07:21.734 --> 00:07:23.601
The salary that you earn
is based on, you know,

00:07:23.601 --> 00:07:25.133
how much they like the show...
-Sure.

00:07:25.133 --> 00:07:27.100
-...and how successful
the show is

00:07:27.100 --> 00:07:29.534
and, you know, how much
they like your contribution

00:07:29.534 --> 00:07:30.767
to the show and everything.

00:07:30.767 --> 00:07:33.968
And I feel like the gift
that you get

00:07:33.968 --> 00:07:37.968
is how much they like you.
-Uh-huh. Yeah, yeah.

00:07:37.968 --> 00:07:39.200
-Wouldn't you think?
-I see. Right.

00:07:39.200 --> 00:07:40.968
So the salary is for the work.

00:07:40.968 --> 00:07:42.267
-For the work.
-The gift is personal.

00:07:42.267 --> 00:07:43.934
-And then the gift is --
Yeah, because it's gratuitous.

00:07:43.934 --> 00:07:45.734
-This is James, the person.
-It's completely gratuitous.

00:07:45.734 --> 00:07:47.434
I don't need a gift really,
you know?

00:07:47.434 --> 00:07:49.901
-Yeah.
-It's a -- It's a gesture.

00:07:49.901 --> 00:07:53.000
It's how much we like you.

00:07:53.000 --> 00:07:56.968
My agents,
they always give me wine.

00:07:56.968 --> 00:07:59.200
-Yeah. Do you like wine?
-I love wine.

00:07:59.200 --> 00:08:01.934
-Perfect.
[ Laughter ]

00:08:01.934 --> 00:08:05.467
-So, this came in an Hermes bag.
-Okay.

00:08:05.467 --> 00:08:08.734
-And I thought, "Wow."
-Yeah.

00:08:08.734 --> 00:08:11.400
-"They really like me."
-Yeah.

00:08:11.400 --> 00:08:13.934
-This is an expensive store.

00:08:13.934 --> 00:08:16.033
And I took it out,

00:08:16.033 --> 00:08:18.968
and it was just

00:08:18.968 --> 00:08:21.100
a square of leather.

00:08:21.100 --> 00:08:23.200
[ Laughter ]

00:08:23.200 --> 00:08:24.701
-Uh-huh.

00:08:24.701 --> 00:08:26.701
-Which I've seen before,
you know?

00:08:26.701 --> 00:08:28.200
I -- You know, like, when --

00:08:28.200 --> 00:08:29.934
We just did a renovation
at our house,

00:08:29.934 --> 00:08:33.434
and I was sent the same thing
as a sample

00:08:33.434 --> 00:08:35.334
to what we might want
to cover the couch with.

00:08:35.334 --> 00:08:37.167
-Okay. Gotcha.
-You know?

00:08:37.167 --> 00:08:39.033
-So it was like --
it was like a leather sample?

00:08:39.033 --> 00:08:42.033
-It was like a leather sample.
It's Hermes.

00:08:42.033 --> 00:08:44.501
I thought maybe it's a sample
for what's possible

00:08:44.501 --> 00:08:45.901
to make a saddle out of.

00:08:45.901 --> 00:08:47.467
[ Laughter ]
-Okay.

00:08:47.467 --> 00:08:49.901
-I really didn't have any idea
what it was.

00:08:49.901 --> 00:08:51.200
-Gotcha.

00:08:51.200 --> 00:08:55.367
-So I called my friend Laura
who works upstairs,

00:08:55.367 --> 00:08:57.667
literally and figuratively.

00:08:57.667 --> 00:08:59.300
And so she came
to my dressing room,

00:08:59.300 --> 00:09:01.534
and I said,
"What the hell is this?"

00:09:01.534 --> 00:09:03.868
Because I was
about to go downstairs...

00:09:03.868 --> 00:09:05.200
-Yeah.

00:09:05.200 --> 00:09:07.767
-...to -- There were going
to be people there that --

00:09:07.767 --> 00:09:09.133
-That had given it to you.

00:09:09.133 --> 00:09:10.834
-Not really.
-Yeah.

00:09:10.834 --> 00:09:12.501
[ Laughter ]

00:09:12.501 --> 00:09:15.534
-Anyway, I said, "What is this?"

00:09:15.534 --> 00:09:18.934
And she said, "Oh. Yes.

00:09:18.934 --> 00:09:20.901
I got the same thing.

00:09:20.901 --> 00:09:22.834
[ Laughter ]

00:09:22.834 --> 00:09:24.634
It's a mouse pad."

00:09:24.634 --> 00:09:27.434
[ Laughter ]

00:09:36.400 --> 00:09:39.868
I mean, I -- Truly, I didn't
even really know what she meant.

00:09:39.868 --> 00:09:41.400
[ Laughter ]

00:09:41.400 --> 00:09:43.334
I don't own a computer.

00:09:43.334 --> 00:09:44.767
-Right.

00:09:46.200 --> 00:09:47.767
-You know, I thought
it was the place

00:09:47.767 --> 00:09:50.801
where your domesticated mouse
goes to the bathroom.

00:09:50.801 --> 00:09:52.200
[ Laughter ]

00:09:52.200 --> 00:09:53.667
-I didn't know -- True story.

00:09:53.667 --> 00:09:56.133
Backstage in your dressing room,
I said,

00:09:56.133 --> 00:09:58.300
"Oh, I'll send you
a link to something."

00:09:58.300 --> 00:10:00.267
And you looked at me --

00:10:00.267 --> 00:10:03.868
You gave me
a real Flaco the Owl look

00:10:03.868 --> 00:10:06.200
when I said "link."

00:10:06.200 --> 00:10:09.234
So I -- I can appreciate
that a mouse pad did not --

00:10:09.234 --> 00:10:10.734
was not straight to your heart.

00:10:10.734 --> 00:10:12.601
[ Laughter ]

00:10:12.601 --> 00:10:14.901
-I feel much better about Flaco.

00:10:14.901 --> 00:10:16.868
[ Laughter ]

00:10:16.868 --> 00:10:19.567
He could do a TV show.

00:10:19.567 --> 00:10:22.000
-James, you know my favorite
thing about having you here?

00:10:22.000 --> 00:10:23.734
-Yeah.
-Because our time is almost up,

00:10:23.734 --> 00:10:25.934
so I want to tell you my --
-It always is.

00:10:25.934 --> 00:10:28.100
[ Laughter ]
-I don't think you've ever --

00:10:28.100 --> 00:10:30.033
Of any guest I've ever had here,

00:10:30.033 --> 00:10:32.767
i don't think you've ever told
two stories.

00:10:32.767 --> 00:10:35.033
[ Laughter ]

00:10:37.467 --> 00:10:39.601
And you don't ever have to.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:10:39.601 --> 00:10:42.200
For my money,
you don't ever have to.

00:10:42.200 --> 00:10:44.868
The one, the only --
James Spader, everybody.

00:10:44.868 --> 00:10:46.801
200th episode of "The Blacklist"

00:10:46.801 --> 00:10:49.834
airs this Sunday, March 19th,
at 10:00 p.m. here on NBC.

00:10:49.834 --> 00:10:51.801
We'll be right back
with Ian McShane.

